# WHAT: single source of truth for the gitleaks version this repo's secret scanning
#       is verified against.
#
# WHY: lefthook.yml used to invoke a bare `gitleaks`, so scan behaviour depended on
#      whichever binary happened to be on PATH. That is not a cosmetic difference --
#      allowlist semantics vary across versions, and "which files actually got
#      scanned" is the entire value of the gate. Local runs used 8.30.1 while a
#      CodeRabbit review sandbox on PR #5 reported 1.7.3, which meant the verified
#      behaviour notes in .gitleaks.toml were true on exactly one machine.
#
# Sourced by scripts/gitleaks-guard.sh, scripts/test-gitleaks-guard.sh and
# .github/workflows/secret-scan.yml. Change versions HERE and nowhere else.
#
# DELIBERATE EXCEPTION: scripts/scan-staged-binaries.sh calls gitleaks directly and is
# NOT routed through the guard. Two reasons, both checked before deciding:
#
#   1. Its canaries read `exit 1` as "gitleaks found the canary token, the toolchain
#      works". The guard also exits 1 when it rejects a version, so wrapping those calls
#      would convert a version failure into a false OK -- the exit-code conflation this
#      whole change set exists to stamp out.
#   2. That script depends on SUBCOMMANDS (`stdin`, --max-archive-depth), not on
#      allowlist semantics, and it already proves those behaviourally per run. Its own
#      self-test stubs an unsupported gitleaks and asserts it fails closed as UNKNOWN;
#      a version floor bolted on top would pre-empt that path and break the assertion
#      without adding coverage.
#
# The guard covers the scans whose CORRECTNESS depends on allowlist semantics -- the
# `secrets` and `secrets-history` steps. The canary covers the scan whose correctness
# depends on subcommand support. They are different checks for different failure modes;
# neither is a substitute for the other.

# --- Local floor ------------------------------------------------------------
# Developer machines must run at least this version. A floor rather than an exact
# match on purpose: gitleaks is brew-managed here, so an exact local pin would break
# every repo on every routine `brew upgrade`, and a gate you habitually bypass
# protects nothing (same reasoning as the advisory lint jobs in lefthook.yml).
#
# The floor alone does NOT prove behaviour -- it only rules out the catastrophic
# old-binary case. Semantic drift ABOVE the floor is caught by the behavioural
# assertions in scripts/test-gitleaks-guard.sh, which run at pre-push. The two
# checks are deliberately different in kind; neither replaces the other.
GITLEAKS_MIN_VERSION=8.30.1

# --- CI pin -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The authoritative scan runs EXACTLY this version, downloaded and checksum-verified,
# so the result does not depend on any developer machine. This may lag or lead the
# floor above; that is fine and expected.
GITLEAKS_PINNED_VERSION=8.30.1

# From https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v8.30.1/gitleaks_8.30.1_checksums.txt
# Verified 2026-08-08. Re-fetch from that URL when bumping GITLEAKS_PINNED_VERSION --
# an unverified download is a supply-chain hole in the tool that guards the repo.
GITLEAKS_PINNED_SHA256_LINUX_X64=551f6fc83ea457d62a0d98237cbad105af8d557003051f41f3e7ca7b3f2470eb
GITLEAKS_PINNED_SHA256_LINUX_ARM64=e4a487ee7ccd7d3a7f7ec08657610aa3606637dab924210b3aee62570fb4b080
